    "message": {
     "content": "### Assessment: Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**In short: It is a standard initial offer (roughly 50th percentile for Google L6 PM), but financially it is not compelling enough to justify leaving Meta IC6.**\n\nHere is why:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Numbers Breakdown\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Google Offer (L6) | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $215,000 | **-$25,000** |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $43,000 (20%) | **+$7,000** |\n| **Annualized Stock (Straight-Line)** | ~$250,000/yr | $200,000/yr ($800k / 4) | **-$50,000** |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | \u2014 | $0 | $0 |\n| **4-Year Average Total Comp** | **~$526,000/yr** | **~$458,000/yr** | **-$68,000/yr** |\n\n---\n\n### 2. The \"Google Vesting Schedule\" Trap\n\nGoogle uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for new-hire equity grants (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** over 4 years):\n\n* **Year 1:** $215,000 base + $43,000 bonus + ~$264,000 stock (at 33%) = **~$522,000**\n* **Year 2:** $215,000 base + $43,000 bonus + ~$264,000 stock (at 33%) = **~$522,000**\n* **Year 3:** $215,000 base + $43,000 bonus + ~$176,000 stock (at 22%) = **~$434,000** *(+ unstacked refreshes)*\n* **Year 4:** $215,000 base + $43,000 bonus + ~$96,000 stock (at 12%) = **~$354,000** *(+ unstacked refreshes)*\n\n**Takeaways:**\n1. **Cash Flow / Base Pay Cut:** You are immediately taking a **$25,000 base pay cut**.\n2. **Year 1 & 2 are flat:** Your Year 1 comp at Google barely matches your current Meta run rate.\n3. **The Year 3\u20134 Cliff:** Although Google gives annual refreshers, they vest over 4 years (25%/yr) and rarely compensate fully for the steep 33% $\\rightarrow$ 12% drop unless you are a top-tier performer receiving outsized grants.\n4. **No Sign-on:** There is no transition cushion for leaving behind unvested Meta stock or bonus timing.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation: Negotiate Firmly\n\nYou hold maximum leverage:\n* You weren\u2019t actively looking; Google recruited you.\n* You are happy and well-compensated at Meta.\n* Google\u2019s initial offer is below your current run rate on a 4-year annualized basis.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counter-Proposal)\n\n| Component | Offer | Target Counter | Rationale / Band Positioning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $215,000 | **$245,000 \u2013 $250,000** | Matches/slightly beats your Meta base; well within Google\u2019s posted $183k\u2013$271k band. |\n| **Equity (GSUs)** | $800,000 | **$1,050,000 \u2013 $1,100,000** | Brings Year 1 equity to ~$350k (33%) and 4-year average to ~$265k+/yr. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets transition friction and forfeit of unvested Meta stock in the immediate cycle. |\n\n**Expected Result:** A counter like this aims for a **Year 1 Total Comp of ~$680k\u2013$700k** (including sign-on) and an ongoing annualized average of **~$570k\u2013$600k/yr**.\n\n---\n\n### Suggested Script / Talking Points for the Recruiter\n\n> *\"Thank you for extending the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team and am excited about the scope of the role. However, looking at the total package, the compensation doesn't make financial sense for me to leave Meta.*\n>\n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and am performing well at Meta, moving for this offer would mean taking a $25,000 cut in base salary and walking away from a $526,000/year run rate for an offer that averages ~$458,000 across four years.*\n>\n> *To bridge this gap and make the move compelling, I would need:*\n> 1. *A base salary of **$245,000** so I am not taking a step back on guaranteed cash.*\n> 2. *An equity grant of **$1,050,000** over 4 years to beat my current trajectory.*\n> 3. *A sign-on bonus of **$80,000** to account for unvested equity and bonus timing.*\n>\n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Bottom Line\n* **Do not accept the current offer.**\n* **Counter with the numbers above.** Google recruiters expect senior PM candidates (especially from Meta) to push back.\n* If Google refuses to budge and won't at least beat your current total comp and match your base, **walk away**\u2014staying at Meta is the better financial choice.",
